# UI-003 — flow-control limit of the per-chunk ack model (raw)

Measured, not assumed. Same `live-probe` as `ui-003-live-probe.log`, with exactly
one line of its burst command patched for the experiment (patch and restore are
part of the harness below), so the burst stream writes TINY pieces:

    yes x | head -c 400000      # line-buffered stdout on a tty => ~2-byte writes
    (probe default)             # head -c 400000 /dev/zero | tr '\0' x  => multi-KiB writes

`yes x` writing 2 bytes at a time makes the server's PTY reader produce ~15-50 byte
`TerminalOutput` frames at a very high frame rate. Every frame must be acknowledged
by one `Client::next_output` call, i.e. per frame the client pays two socket reads
(frame prefix + body) plus one `FlowControl` write, while the server produces a
frame with a single PTY `read`. The client therefore cannot match that frame rate,
the server's per-stream credit window (256 KiB) or its 8192-message outbound queue
fills, and the server closes the whole connection (`Subscriber::overflow`,
`flow_control_overflow` / "outbound channel full").

Consequence: this is a property of the protocol's per-chunk ack model plus the
server's output bounds, not of the UI-003 migration. The four transcripts below are
the committed bulk-pull `collect_events` data plane (`apps/linux/src/api/data.rs`
@ UI-003) and every one of them ends on the same server line, `stream 2 outbound
channel full`: the stream's 8192-message bounded outbound queue
(`OUTBOUND_QUEUE_MESSAGES`, `crates/relayable-server/src/connection.rs:38`) fills,
`Subscriber::overflow` runs, and the connection closes; the four runs delivered
296,833 / 64,573 / 86,598 / 47,041 bytes on the burst stream before that (≈290, 63,
85, 46 KiB). The credit window (256 KiB) is the other bound in the same code
(`credit exhausted` in `crates/relayable-server/src/session/subscriber.rs`); that
line does not appear in these transcripts.

The earlier stream-targeted `next_output` pump structure is **not** preserved, so it
is not compared here and no parity claim is made. `/tmp/opencode/ui003-probe/data.rs.variantA`
is byte-identical to `data.rs.orig` (same md5), that structure's code is deleted
between `494917f` and `8d84a84`, and no transcript of it survives — the comparison
cannot be re-run. What *is* supported is narrower: (a) the structural argument for the
bulk-pull read (the pump drains every attached stream to empty before and after its one
bounded read, so no chunk waits on another stream), and (b) the independent verifier's
finding during review that raising `DATA_READ_WINDOW` from 1 ms to 100 ms makes the
acceptance burst fail through the credit path (`credit exhausted`) — i.e. the read
window is load-bearing, not incidental.

It is **not** part of the acceptance gate: the gate burst writes in multi-KiB blocks
(a real command's output granularity) and passes deterministically
(`ui-003-live-probe.log`).

Reproduce (`/tmp/opencode/ui003-probe/flow.sh`):

```
# patch the burst command for the run:
sed -i "s|head -c {BURST_BYTES} /dev/zero | tr '\\0' x;|yes x | head -c {BURST_BYTES};|" \
    apps/linux/src/bin/live-probe.rs
cd apps/linux && CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true cargo build --locked --bin live-probe
# scratch server (own socket + own state dir), then:
RELAYABLE_SERVER=<sock> timeout 60 cargo run --locked --bin live-probe
# restore the burst command and rebuild afterwards
```

